became president, how they got there, and what happened after the promotion."],[18.75,"One new president's dad even has to bring out a lamp."],[21.6,"It starts with John Adams, George Washington's vice president."],[24.91,"For eight years, Washington leaned on his cabinet while Adams complained about how insignificant his own job was."],[30.93,"Then Washington retired, and Adams won the election of 1796."],[35.88,"There was one awkward detail."],[37.75,"Under the original electoral system, the runner-up became vice president."],[41.78,"So Adams got Thomas Jefferson, the man who had just run against him."],[45.33,"His defeated opponent was now his deputy."],[47.64,"Four years later, Jefferson ran against him again."],[49.92,"This time Adams lost, but Jefferson still had somebody to beat."],[53.9,"His own running mate, Aaron Burr, had received exactly as many electoral votes."],[57.74,"Back then, electors didn't cast separate votes for president and vice president."],[62.14,"The tie went to the House of Representatives, which took thirty-six ballots to choose Jefferson."],[66.73,"America had almost elected the running mate instead of the intended president."],[70.44,"Jefferson finally took office in 1801 and later approved the Louisiana Purchase."],[75.3,"The Twelfth Amendment changed the electoral system afterward."],[78.97,"After Jefferson, another sitting vice president wouldn't win the presidency until Martin Van Buren in 1836."],[85.95,"Andrew Jackson's trusted ally looked like a natural successor."],[89.32,"Then, just months into his presidency, the Panic of 1837 hit."],[94.07,"Banks failed, businesses collapsed, and the country fell into a depression."],[98.46,"Van Buren inherited troubles that had been building before he arrived."],[102.02,"Voters still handed him the bill."],[104.3,"In 1840, they replaced him with William Henry Harrison."],[107.92,"Harrison's vice president was John Tyler, and Tyler's promotion came much faster."]],"words":[["one",0,0.0],["be